  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 321 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThe aforementioned upper-level disturbance should move across the \nFlorida peninsula on Sunday, bringing the wettest period of the \nforecast. Increasing large-scale ascent, cooler mid-level \ntemperatures and a deep moisture profile should support \nwidespread shower and thunderstorm development through much of the\nday, with PoPs in the 80-90% range. Heavy rainfall and potential \nflooding will be the main concerns, with WPC already placing the \nEast Coast metro areas under a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) of \nFlash flooding on Sunday and Monday. In fact, guidance shows the \nhighest convective coverage could be most likely along the East \nCoast. However, this will ultimately be very dependent on the \ntiming, track and strength of the feature as it moves across the \narea. \n\nDESI 500mb heights ensemble cluster analysis still shows a primary \ncluster with a weaker system that takes a more northerly track, \nwhile the other three clusters favor some flavor of a deeper system \ntaking a more southerly track. The more northerly track would take \nthe higher rainfall totals north across northern-central FL, while\nthe more southerly tracks would keep the heavy rainfall \nconcentrated across central and south FL. Among the global \nensembles, the GEFS moves the system eastward faster, while the \nENS shows the disturbance, and thus its impacts, lingering into \nMonday. As such, the ENS solution is also the wetter of the \npossible options. As a result of these slight guidance \ndiscrepancies, there is still somewhat low-to-medium confidence \nregarding the forecast for Sunday and Monday, but we erred on the \nside of the more southerly solutions and kept high PoPs gradually \ntapering off from west to east on Monday night. \n\nConfidence in the forecast declines further as we head into the \nmiddle of next week. Mid-level ridging will look to build briefly \nacross the area, favoring a brief dry out across the region\nTuesday and Wednesday. However, later in the week the global \nmodels try to develop the next upper level trough and surface low\nover the Rockies. This complex would gradually move eastward and \npotentially bring a cold front across the FL peninsula late in the\nweek. Considerable spread remains in the timing and strength of \nthis solution, and we will continue to monitor the evolution of \nthe forecast as we approach next week. \n\nTemperatures will cool down a bit Sunday-Monday with the increased \nstorminess and cloud cover, with highs in the mid 80s. Lows will \nslide into the mid 60s across the interior, and the low 70s along\nthe coasts.",
